        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#same-company" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Same Company, Different Tools</a>
          <a href="#batteries" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. M18 vs ONE+</a>
          <a href="#where-it-shows" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Where the Money Shows</a>
          <a href="#where-it-doesnt" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Where It Doesn't</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Real Cost Comparison</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Verdict</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Both brands belong to Techtronic Industries, which makes this the rare comparison where the honest framing is not rivalry but market segmentation. They are aimed at different people, and most buyers are more clearly one of those people than they think.
        </p>

        <h2 id="same-company" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Same Company, Different Tools
        </h2>

        <p>
          Both Milwaukee and Ryobi are owned by Techtronic Industries, the Hong Kong-based group that also owns Hart and AEG. This fact circulates online as though it settles the question — same company, so surely the tools are the same underneath.
        </p>

        <p>
          It does not settle anything. The two brands operate as separate businesses with separate engineering teams, separate component supply chains and, most importantly, separate quality and cost targets. Milwaukee is positioned squarely at trades who will use a tool every working day; Ryobi is positioned at homeowners and light users who will not. Everything about how the tools are designed follows from that.
        </p>

        <p>
          What the shared ownership does explain is why the two ranges avoid competing directly. Ryobi does not chase Milwaukee's core trade categories, and Milwaukee does not make the household oddities — glue guns, misters, inflators, bug zappers — that populate the ONE+ catalogue. That is deliberate segmentation, and it works well for buyers because it means the choice is genuinely about which kind of user you are rather than about which brand happens to be better at any given moment in the product cycle.
        </p>

        <h2 id="batteries" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. M18 vs ONE+
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Milwaukee M18 and M12</h3>

        <p>
          M18 is the flagship: over two hundred and fifty tools, deep coverage of every trade category, and REDLINK electronics that manage communication between pack and tool to prevent overload and overheating. HIGH OUTPUT and FORGE packs sit at the top of the range and are what allow M18 tools to do work that used to need a cord.
        </p>

        <p>
          M12 is the compact platform and it is genuinely underrated. For plumbing, electrical, HVAC and any work in confined spaces, an M12 tool is often the better tool rather than the cheaper one — smaller, lighter, and easier to get into a joist bay. Many trades carry both.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ryobi ONE+</h3>

        <p>
          ONE+ has the single most remarkable compatibility story in the industry. Every ONE+ tool made since 1996 takes every ONE+ battery, which is nearly three decades of backwards compatibility that no competitor comes close to matching. If you inherited a drill and a charger from a relative, a tool you buy tomorrow will run on it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The range is also the largest anywhere — over three hundred tools — and much of that breadth is in places professional brands do not go. Cordless glue guns, inflators, misting fans, wet-dry vacs, hybrid lights that also run on mains. For a household, that catalogue is genuinely useful in a way a trade-focused range is not.
        </p>

        <p>
          The distinction that matters within Ryobi is <strong>HP brushless versus standard</strong>. The HP line uses brushless motors and better electronics, and it is dramatically closer to professional performance than the base tools. Anyone buying into ONE+ today should be buying HP wherever it exists.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Battery Capacity and Charging</h3>

        <p>
          Milwaukee's pack range runs from compact 2.0Ah units up to 12.0Ah FORGE packs, and the electronics in each communicate continuously with the tool. That communication is what allows an M18 tool to draw hard without cooking itself, and it is a genuine engineering advantage rather than a marketing one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ryobi's packs cover a similar nominal capacity range at a fraction of the price, and the HIGH PERFORMANCE line narrows the gap considerably. What they lack is the same depth of protection electronics, which shows up as faster ageing under heavy cycling rather than as any immediate difference in use. For a homeowner charging a pack once a month, that distinction is academic.
        </p>

        <p>
          Charging is a clearer win for Milwaukee. Its rapid chargers bring a large pack back in well under an hour and manage temperature while doing it. Ryobi's standard chargers are slow enough that owning several batteries is effectively mandatory, though at ONE+ prices that is an easy problem to solve.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-it-shows" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Where the Money Shows
        </h2>

        <p>
          The premium is real and it is worth being specific about what it buys, because "better quality" is not a useful answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sustained output under load.</strong> This is the big one. Both brands will drive a lag bolt. The difference appears on the fiftieth consecutive bolt, when the Milwaukee is still delivering the same torque and the Ryobi has slowed, warmed up, and started backing off to protect itself. Peak performance figures do not capture this at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Thermal management.</strong> Related, and the underlying cause. Milwaukee's motors, electronics and housings are designed to shed heat over long duty cycles. Ryobi's are designed for intermittent use with cooling gaps between. Neither is wrong for its intended user.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Durability of the mechanism.</strong> Chucks that stay concentric after a year, clutches that keep their calibration, triggers with a longer switch life. These are not visible in a store and they are precisely what a professional notices in month eight.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Battery life and consistency.</strong> Milwaukee packs hold capacity better over many cycles, and the electronics communicate with the tool to prevent the deep discharges that kill cells. A ONE+ pack in heavy use ages faster.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Service and parts.</strong> Milwaukee is genuinely repairable, with a service network, published parts diagrams and spares held in stock, so a worn switch or a damaged chuck is a repair rather than a write-off. Ryobi tools are, in practical terms, replaced rather than repaired — parts exist on paper but the labour of fitting them usually exceeds the price of a new tool, which is a reasonable design choice at that price rather than a failing.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-it-doesnt" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Where It Doesn't
        </h2>

        <p>
          There is a long list of jobs where the premium buys you nothing you will ever perceive.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hanging pictures, assembling flat-pack furniture, building a deck over three weekends, drilling a few dozen holes a year, driving screws into softwood. In all of these, a Ryobi HP drill does exactly what a Milwaukee does, and the tool spends the other three hundred and sixty days in a drawer either way.
        </p>

        <p>
          The same applies to most of the household categories where Ryobi has no Milwaukee equivalent. An inflator, a glue gun, a hand vacuum, a misting fan — these are convenience tools where the ONE+ version is not a compromise, it is the only option on a battery you already own.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also applies to secondary tools for professionals. Plenty of tradespeople run Milwaukee for their core kit and keep a Ryobi for the tool they use twice a year, or the one they are willing to leave in a customer's basement overnight. That is a perfectly rational split, and it is different from splitting your main platform.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">A Note on Outdoor Equipment</h3>

        <p>
          Cordless outdoor power equipment is one area where the usual logic inverts. Ryobi has invested heavily here and its 40V outdoor line — mowers, blowers, trimmers, chainsaws — is genuinely competitive with anything on the market, including tools from brands that cost considerably more. For a homeowner with a suburban lot, the Ryobi outdoor range is one of the strongest value propositions in the whole cordless world.
        </p>

        <p>
          Milwaukee's outdoor equipment runs on M18 and is aimed at landscaping crews rather than households. It is excellent and it is priced accordingly. Unless you are cutting grass for a living, this is a category where the premium brand has nothing to offer you that the value brand does not already do well.
        </p>

        <p>
          Note that Ryobi's 40V outdoor platform is separate from ONE+ and shares no batteries with it, which is the one place where the brand's compatibility story breaks down. Plan for two battery systems if you buy into both.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. The Real Cost Comparison
        </h2>

        <p>
          Compare the full entry cost rather than the drill price. A four-tool Milwaukee M18 kit with two HIGH OUTPUT batteries and a charger typically lands at three to four times the price of a comparable Ryobi ONE+ HP kit. That multiple holds fairly consistently across categories.
        </p>

        <p>
          The right way to think about it is cost per hour of use. A professional running tools forty hours a week reaches the duty cycles where Milwaukee's durability pays for itself within the first year, and then keeps paying. A homeowner using tools thirty hours a year will not reach that point in a decade, so the premium is simply money spent on capability that never gets exercised.
        </p>

        <p>
          Batteries change the arithmetic more than tools do. Because ONE+ packs are so much cheaper, a Ryobi user can carry four or five batteries for the price of one Milwaukee HIGH OUTPUT pack. For occasional use, having spare charged packs matters more than having better packs.
        </p>

        <p>
          One genuine warning on the Ryobi side: the enormous catalogue is designed to make you keep buying. Tools that cost forty dollars because you already have the battery add up faster than anyone expects, and a shed full of single-purpose ONE+ tools can quietly cost more than a focused professional kit would have.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What About Resale?</h3>

        <p>
          Milwaukee holds its value unusually well on the second-hand market, and the used market for M18 tools is deep and active. A tool bought new and sold after three years of moderate use often returns a substantial fraction of its price, which meaningfully narrows the effective cost gap for anyone who buys deliberately and sells on.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ryobi has almost no resale value, and there is no point pretending otherwise. Tools are cheap enough new that nobody buys them used at a price worth the trouble of selling. Factor that in only if you are the kind of buyer who actually sells old tools rather than accumulating them, which most people are not.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buying used Milwaukee is, incidentally, one of the better routes into a professional platform on a limited budget. A used M18 drill and impact driver with a couple of tired batteries costs little more than a new Ryobi HP kit, and replacement packs can be added over time. The risk is that you cannot see a tool's duty history, so buy from someone who can tell you what it was used for.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">6. The Verdict</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-200 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Milwaukee</strong> if you earn money with tools. The premium buys sustained output, thermal headroom, mechanism durability and a repair path, and every one of those is measured in duty cycles that a professional reaches and a homeowner does not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-200 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Ryobi HP</strong> if you maintain a house. Three hundred tools on a battery platform that has been compatible since 1996, at a third of the price, with brushless performance that is genuinely close for intermittent work.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-200 m-0">
            The one combination that does not work is buying the cheap platform for demanding daily work, or the expensive one for a tool that lives in a drawer. Be honest about which user you are and the answer is usually obvious.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Assuming shared ownership means shared quality</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Techtronic owns both, but the brands use different motors, electronics, housings and quality targets. Common ownership tells you nothing about whether two tools perform alike.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying Milwaukee for occasional use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A tool used ten hours a year will never repay a professional-grade premium. The durability you paid for is measured in duty cycles you will not reach.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying Ryobi for daily production work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The failure mode is not a dramatic breakdown — it is a tool that gets slower, hotter and less accurate under sustained load, and then dies at the least convenient moment.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Comparing the flagship to the base model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ryobi's HP brushless line is far closer to Milwaukee than its blue-badge budget tools are. Compare like with like or the gap looks bigger than it is.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Splitting across both platforms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Two chargers, two battery stocks, two sets of spares. Whatever you save on the cheap tool, you lose in duplicated batteries.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi made by the same company as Milwaukee?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. Both are owned by Techtronic Industries, along with Hart and AEG. They are run as separate businesses with separate engineering teams and quality targets, so the shared ownership does not mean the tools are related in any meaningful way.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee worth the extra money over Ryobi?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">If you use tools daily for a living, yes — the difference in sustained performance, heat management and service life is real and it shows up within months. For occasional homeowner use, no. You will not reach the duty cycles where the premium pays back.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Milwaukee and Ryobi batteries interchangeable?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No. M18 and ONE+ are physically and electronically incompatible despite the shared parent company. Third-party adapters exist and are a bad idea — they bypass the communication between pack and tool that protects both.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi good enough for professional use?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For light trades, occasional site work and secondary tools, the HP brushless line is genuinely capable. For daily production work it is not, and the reason is thermal management under sustained load rather than peak power.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do Ryobi tools last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">In homeowner use, many years — plenty of ONE+ tools are still working a decade on. In daily professional use, expect noticeably shorter life than a professional-grade tool, particularly on anything that runs continuously.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi ONE+ still compatible with old batteries?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, and it is the platform's headline strength. Every ONE+ tool since 1996 shares the same battery interface, so a modern tool takes an old pack and vice versa. No other platform has maintained compatibility that long.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Which has more tools available?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Ryobi ONE+, by a wide margin — over three hundred tools, including a great many niche and household items that no professional brand makes. Milwaukee's M18 range is deep in trade tools specifically.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy Ryobi HP or standard Ryobi?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">HP, almost always. The brushless motors run cooler, last longer and deliver meaningfully more power for a modest premium. The standard blue-badge tools are for genuinely light occasional use only.</p>
          </div>
